WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.480
Film Courage: What are your thoughts on Hollywood
stealing an unknown writer’s idea?

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.040
Does this happen a lot?

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:11.839
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: I think
it happens a lot less than people believe

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:16.160
because my writing partner and I used to say
we’d make more money off of the lawsuit

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.949
than we would on the screenplay, you know?

00:00:18.949 --> 00:00:20.699
Go ahead and steal.

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:26.960
It would be such a pleasure for you to steal
our idea that it means it’s something good.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:33.220
But writers do…writers ask me “Okay, what
is this process of copyright before I let

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.220
anybody read it?”

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.730
I was like “Okay, you can copyright the
script.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:42.570
It takes a process, it’s money or you can
register at the guild.”

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:48.370
Or some say that by the mere existence of
creating it, it’s a copyright so there are

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:52.820
all sorts of factors but what I’ve read
is that the copyright would protect you more

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:54.260
in court.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.080
But the thing of that is you never own a copyright
of a screenplay because the minute you sell

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.780
it it becomes a work for hire.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:05.000
So you always have to sign over your rights
to the company so that was given away a long

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.290
time ago in guild negotiations that the writer
never owns the copy of the script.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:22.120
So it’s okay to do that but when you see
on the cover ‘Registered WGAW #126555,’

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:29.990
that’s just a giveaway of like an amateur
that is afraid of my stuff being stolen, you

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.330
know what I mean?

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.430
No professional writes that on the cover of
a script.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:41.200
I think professionals expect you to do your
due diligence and I think the more chance

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.630
of something being stolen is a pitch because
that is out in the ether you know?

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:53.420
I was saying, my writing partner and I would
go on these pitch fests that their agent would

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:59.460
send us on to like 12 companies and we’d
do the same pitch around town and at 2 o’clock

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.430
we’ve got something with this person.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:06.250
And our agent/manager would ask us “How
did it go?”

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.490
And we’d say “Well…it didn’t…they
didn’t, their eyes didn’t light up.”

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:13.120
And they’d go “Maybe you should switch
it and change this element.”

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:19.320
So from the afternoon on we changed a different
version of the thing but my point is it’s

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.030
all a living, breathing thing.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:27.670
Somebody could wake up the next morning and
go…now they might not be the exact pitch

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:31.340
but let’s say an element of it and you know
I have an idea?

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.290
And that works in, what do you do about that?

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:39.240
But you can’t be that way where you think
everybody is going to steal your stuff.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.860
It’s just not…you can be smart and when
you send out your script you should keep track

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:51.210
in a log or journal or spreadsheet who is
reading the script every time out.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.930
If you don’t have an agent or manager who
that’s their job obviously they will know

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:55.930
that.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:56.930
It will be through an official channel.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.720
But if somebody says “Oh give me the script,
I work at the company.”

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.470
That goes down, who read it?

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:02.470
When?

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:07.209
Because these are important things to know
who, you don’t want to send…you want to

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:08.320
know who is reading it.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.270
That’s important too because if it starts
getting bad feedback you never hear about

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:17.050
it and then suddenly you’re like “Why
didn’t I get a meeting with that company?”

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.750
Because people are talking now.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.270
In the old days it would go in a database
and there would be coverage in the studio

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:26.650
and you couldn’t fool them because they’d
go [imitates typing into a computer] “Oh…we

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.470
read that…two years ago.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.810
They’re trying to pawn that off on us again?”

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:37.989
So I wouldn’t be too fearful and there was
a few stories about outright theft but I think

00:03:37.989 --> 00:03:43.940
it’s so…you can’t be afraid to send
your stuff out.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:48.690
And it’s sort of an arrogant idea “Well
my idea is so genius that they’re going

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:49.690
to steal it!”

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:54.370
Like a studio doesn’t have enough amazing
ideas already and your might be the one they

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.310
take but it would be a good lawsuit.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:04:02.750
Film Courage: But that being said don’t
be irresponsible sending it out.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.640
Log the date, the place, the address even?

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.690
Mark: Oh yeah, keep it all because you want
to keep track of who is reading your stuff.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.239
You want to keep in control of that.

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:19.620
That is why when writers post their stuff
like a script up on their web page I would

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.059
say don’t do that.

00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:22.490
You don’t know who is reading it.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:23.889
You don’t know who is stealing your ideas.

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:25.629
You have no way of tracking that.

00:04:25.629 --> 00:04:30.229
If you’re afraid of that that’s even worse
because somebody is just going to your website

00:04:30.229 --> 00:04:35.900
and I know it’s a way to promote your stuff
but that is not the way to have your full

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.979
script on your website for someone just to
read.

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:43.310
Who is reading it?

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:44.169
You want to keep track of that.

